window.NTI=window.NTI||{};(window.NTI.docs=window.NTI.docs||{})["ansible-60-automation-br-manual"]={"html":"ANSIBLE FOUNDATIONS — FROM MARKDOWN, FULL DETAIL<div class=\"sheet cover\"><div class=\"ckick\">Automation Control Manual</div><div class=\"stamp\">ANSIBLE</div><h1 id=\"automationmanual\">AUTOMATION<br />MANUAL</h1><hr /><div class=\"sub\">From Remote Execution to Declarative Infrastructure — <b>Foundations</b><br />Full chapter detail — rendered from content Markdown</div><div class=\"vols\"><div class=\"volcard on\">VOL 1 — FOUNDATIONS<small>this book</small></div><div class=\"volcard\">VOL 2 — ENGINEERING</div><div class=\"volcard\">VOL 3 — PRODUCTION</div></div><div class=\"cver\">A4 · Playwright · Background ON · Eye Palette · Rendered from content Markdown · foundations · VOL 1 — FOUNDATIONS · نسخة عربية</div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Contents</div><h1 id=\"table-of-contents\">TABLE OF CONTENTS</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">CORE</span></div></div><div><div><a href=\"#ch-01\"><span class=\"tnum\">01</span><span class=\"tp\">Why Automation</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>03</a><a href=\"#ch-02\"><span class=\"tnum\">02</span><span class=\"tp\">Agentless Architecture</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>07</a><a href=\"#ch-03\"><span class=\"tnum\">03</span><span class=\"tp\">Inventory and Targeting</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>11</a></div><div><a href=\"#ch-04\"><span class=\"tnum\">04</span><span class=\"tp\">First Playbooks</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>16</a><a href=\"#ch-05\"><span class=\"tnum\">05</span><span class=\"tp\">Modules and Idempotency</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>20</a><a href=\"#cheatsheet\"><span class=\"tnum\">A</span><span class=\"tp\">Cheatsheet</span><span class=\"dots\"></span>24</a></div></div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">How to read</span>Chapters render the full content Markdown: problem, architecture, targets, playbooks, idempotency, labs, interview checks.</div><p>Version anchor: Pinned to ansible-core 2.16+ with the community package; FQCN in every example..</p><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">The anchor lane — reused every chapter</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">COMMAND</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">LOAD INVENTORY</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">PLAY TO TASK</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk b-cyan\">MODULE</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk b-ok\">RESULT</span></div></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">2</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 01 · Foundations</div><h1 id=\"why-automation-beats-scripts\">WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">5 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"panel ar-panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">🇪🇬 لماذا <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Automation</span>؟ شرح عربي</div><div class=\"ar\">«<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Ansible</span>» أداة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Automation</span> مفتوحة المصدر (<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Open-Source</span>) تُستخدم أساسًا في <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Configuration Management</span> (إدارة الإعدادات) و<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Infrastructure Provisioning</span> (تجهيز البنية التحتية). الفكرة: تصف <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Desired State</span> (الحالة المطلوبة) في ملف <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">YAML</span>، و«Ansible» يصل إليها بنفسه. <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Script</span> العادي يقول «نفّذ الأمر مجددًا»، بينما «Ansible» يقارن المطلوب بالواقع ثم يقرر: <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">changed</span> أم <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ok</span>. البنية التحتية تُبنى بـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Terraform</span>، والإعداد داخل الأجهزة مسؤولية «Ansible» — تسليم واضح لا ازدواج فيه.</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Automation</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Desired State</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">YAML</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Terraform</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">changed / ok</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT — The automation problem</div><p>Manual operation scales linearly with machines and fails quietly. Shell scripts capture *commands* but not *intent*: which machines, what state, what if state already exists, how to verify, how to retry. Configuration drifts. Knowledge lives in runbooks and heads.</p><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Manual loop vs declarative converge</div><div class=\"grid2\"><div class=\"zone\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Manual</div><p>Operator → SSH for-loop → hosts one by one → drift accumulates → re-run repeats side effects</p></div><div class=\"zone\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Declarative</div><p>Playbook → control node → inventory select → compare desired vs current → CHANGED or OK, audited</p></div></div></div><p>Ansible addresses this by declaring <b>desired state</b> for <b>selected targets</b> and converging each target toward that state, reporting whether change was needed.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHY — Why Ansible exists</div><p>Problem it solves: repeatable, auditable, idempotent configuration and orchestration without per-host agents or custom orchestration code for simple cases.</p><p>Where it fits:</p><div class=\"code\">Provisioning (Terraform) → Configuration (Ansible) → Orchestration (K8s) → Verification (Observability)</div><p>Ansible owns the configuration layer: OS, packages, files, services, users, application config — on bare metal, VMs, cloud instances, network devices where supported.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">3</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 01 · Foundations (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"why-automation-beats-scripts-2\">WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">5 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHERE DOES IT FIT — Ecosystem lane</div><ul><li>Script (Bash): \"run this command again\" → no convergence check</li><li>Image baking (Packer): snapshot state → not day-2 config</li><li>Provisioner (Terraform): creates infrastructure → hands off to Ansible for inside-OS config</li><li>Orchestrator (K8s): schedules containers → Ansible can provision/configure K8s nodes themselves</li></ul><p>Decision tree (preview, detailed in PDF3):</p><div class=\"code\">WHAT ARE YOU AUTOMATING? → Infrastructure → Terraform/OpenTofu\n                        → Host/app config → Ansible</div><p>Same question, three answers — the comparison that decides the tool:</p><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Question</th><th>Shell loop</th><th>Ansible</th></tr><tr><td>Which machines?</td><td>Hosts hardcoded in the loop</td><td>Inventory selects by group and pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Already correct?</td><td>Runs again blindly</td><td>Reports <code>OK</code>, changes nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Proof it worked?</td><td>Exit code of the last host</td><td>Per-host recap, every host</td></tr><tr><td>Repeat next week?</td><td>Hope nothing changed</td><td>Converge again with the same file</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">4</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 01 · Foundations (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"why-automation-beats-scripts-3\">WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">5 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">TERRAFORM VS ANSIBLE — Provision vs configure</div><p>Terraform <b>provisions</b> infrastructure (creates it: VPCs, VMs, DNS, load balancers) and tracks it in a state file. Ansible <b>configures</b> what already exists (OS, packages, files, services, users) and converges it on every run, keeping no state of its own.</p><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Terraform / OpenTofu</th><th>Ansible</th></tr><tr><td>Owns</td><td>Infrastructure lifecycle (create, change, destroy)</td><td>Inside-OS desired state (configure, converge)</td></tr><tr><td>State</td><td>State file (<code>terraform.tfstate</code>) maps config to real resources</td><td>Stateless; compares desired vs current on the host each run</td></tr><tr><td>Runs</td><td><code>plan</code> previews infra diff; <code>apply</code> changes infra</td><td><code>--check --diff</code> previews config diff; run converges hosts</td></tr><tr><td>Re-run cost</td><td>Re-plans against APIs; safe but slow</td><td>Re-converges; <code>OK</code> where already correct, fast</td></tr><tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Provider creds + sensitive outputs</td><td>Vault-encrypted vars, <code>no_log</code>, become scoping</td></tr></table></div><p>Combined workflow (the normal production shape):</p><div class=\"code\">Terraform apply → outputs IPs/hostnames → generated inventory → Ansible configures OS/apps → services verified</div><div class=\"code\">terraform output -json &gt; tf-out.json   # ips of new VMs\nansible-playbook -i inventories/prod site.yml</div><p>Handoff rule: Terraform hands <b>addresses</b>, Ansible takes <b>responsibility inside the machine</b>. Generate inventory from Terraform outputs (dynamic inventory plugin or <code>terraform output</code> templated into <code>hosts.yml</code>) — never hand-copy IPs between tools.</p><p>Anti-patterns:</p><ul><li>Creating VPCs/VMs with Ansible modules → duplicates Terraform state, two sources of truth</li><li>Configuring apps with Terraform <code>provisioner</code> blocks beyond bootstrap → provisioners run once at create time, never converge drift; hand off to Ansible after boot</li><li>Storing Terraform outputs with secrets in plain files → mark <code>sensitive = true</code>, feed Ansible via Vault</li></ul><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">5</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 01 · Foundations (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"why-automation-beats-scripts-4\">WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">5 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">HOW IT WORKS — High-level loop</div><div class=\"code\">USER → ansible-playbook → CONTROL NODE (inventory, vars, tasks) → SSH/WinRM → MANAGED NODE (module) → TARGET STATE → RESULT (OK/CHANGED/FAILED)</div><div class=\"taskhead\">PRACTICAL — Not yet executable (intentional)</div><p>This chapter builds motivation. Execution begins in Ch 02–03. No commands to mis-run yet.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT CAN FAIL — Misunderstanding automation</div><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely Cause</th><th>Prevention</th></tr><tr><td>Automation reintroduces drift</td><td>Scripts without idempotency</td><td>Teach CHANGED vs OK early (Ch 05)</td></tr><tr><td>Over-automation of infra as config</td><td>Using Ansible to create VPCs</td><td>Handoff callout: provision vs configure</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"taskhead\">REAL-WORLD CONNECTION</div><p>SRE uses Ansible to converge fleet after patch window: same playbook against staging then production, diff mode first, check mode for approval.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">Interview check</div><div class=\"m-exam\"><span class=\"tag\">Interview</span><b>Why not just run shell scripts via SSH loop?</b><br />Scripts describe commands; automation must describe targets, desired state, convergence check, error handling, and audit result. Ansible's model adds selection, declaration, and verification around the transport that a loop lacks.</div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Next</span>We know *why* to automate. Next: *how* Ansible reaches machines — agentless architecture.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">6</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 02 · Architecture</div><h1 id=\"agentless-control-to-managed\">AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">6 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"panel ar-panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">🇪🇬 <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Architecture</span>: شرح عربي</div><div class=\"ar\"><span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Architecture</span> من مكوّنين: <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Control Node</span> (جهازك أو <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Server</span> Linux مثبّت عليه «Ansible») و<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Managed Nodes</span> (<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Servers</span> المستهدفة مثل Ubuntu / CentOS). الاتصال عبر <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">SSH</span> في Linux و<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">WinRM/SSH</span> في Windows بمفتاحي <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Public/Private Key Pair</span>، والمتطلب الوحيد على <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Servers</span> Linux هو بيئة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Python</span>. لا <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Agents</span> على المستهدَف — «Ansible» يتصل، ينفّذ <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Module</span>، يعيد <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">JSON</span>، ثم يغادر. على Windows استخدم بيئة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">WSL</span> كـ Control Node.</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Control Node</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Managed Nodes</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">SSH</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">WinRM</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Python</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Agentless</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT — Agentless architecture</div><p>Ansible requires no permanently running agent on managed nodes. The <b>control node</b> holds automation content and initiates connections; <b>managed nodes</b> receive module payloads and execute them.</p><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Agentless execution path</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk b-red\">CONTROL NODE<small>playbook · inventory · vars</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">SSH / WinRM<small>connection plugin</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">MANAGED NODE<small>module runs · JSON back</small></span></div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Linux<small>OpenSSH + Python 3</small></span><span class=\"blk\">Windows<small>WinRM + PowerShell</small></span></div></div><div class=\"code\">┌───────────────────┐\n│ CONTROL NODE      │\n│ ansible-playbook  │\n│ inventory         │\n│ variables         │\n│ modules (FQCN)    │\n└─────────┬─────────┘\n          │ SSH / WinRM (connection plugins)\n  ┌───────┴────────┐\n  ↓                ↓\nHOST 01 (Linux)  HOST 02 (Windows via WinRM)</div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHY — Why agentless</div><p>Problem: agent lifecycle (install, upgrade, cert rotation) is its own fleet problem. Agentless trades persistent presence for on-demand execution: simpler bootstrap, no agent fleet to patch, works over standard transports already present.</p><p>Tradeoff callout:</p><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Why this exists</span>Agents give push-without-SSH but require ongoing fleet.\nAgentless gives immediate start but requires reachable transport + credentials per run.\nChoose agentless when SSH/WinRM already managed; choose agented when pull/continuous drift correction required.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">7</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 02 · Architecture (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"agentless-control-to-managed-2\">AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">6 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHERE DOES IT FIT — Execution model</div><div class=\"code\">COMMAND → LOAD CONFIG (ansible.cfg) → LOAD INVENTORY → SELECT HOSTS → LOAD VARIABLES/FACTS → PROCESS PLAY → EXECUTE TASK → RUN MODULE → RETURN RESULT → NEXT TASK</div><p>This lane is the visual anchor reused every chapter.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">HOW IT WORKS</div><ul><li><b>Control node:</b> where <code>ansible</code> / <code>ansible-playbook</code> runs. Needs Python 3.11+, collections, inventory, <code>ansible.cfg</code>.</li><li><b>Managed node:</b> target host. Needs Python (most modules) or PowerShell (Windows), SSH/WinRM reachable, privilege path (<code>become</code>).</li><li><b>Transport:</b> connection plugin. Default <code>ssh</code> (Linux), <code>winrm</code>/<code>psrp</code> (Windows). Data sent: module payload + args; result returned as JSON.</li><li><b>Module execution:</b> module runs on managed node (or locally if <code>delegate_to: localhost</code>), reports <code>changed</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>skipped</code>.</li></ul><p>Config loading order (callout):</p><div class=\"code\">ansible.cfg precedence: ANSIBLE_CONFIG env → ./ansible.cfg → ~/.ansible.cfg → /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg</div><div class=\"taskhead\">PRACTICAL — Verify control node</div><div class=\"code\"></div><div class=\"code\">$ ansible --version\nansible [core 2.16.x] ...</div><p>Meaning: confirms control node install. Target: local. Internal: loads config, no inventory needed. Expected: version + config path. Failure: <code>command not found</code> → install <code>ansible-core</code> + <code>ansible</code>.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">8</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 02 · Architecture (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"agentless-control-to-managed-3\">AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">6 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WSL SETUP — Control node on Windows</div><p>Windows cannot run Ansible natively. WSL2 + Ubuntu is the supported control node: a real Linux kernel where <code>ansible-playbook</code>, Python, and OpenSSH behave exactly like CI.</p><div class=\"code\">wsl --install -d Ubuntu        # Windows Terminal (admin): installs WSL2 + Ubuntu\nsudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip pipx openssh-client git\npipx install ansible            # ansible + ansible-playbook on PATH\npipx inject ansible ansible-lint\nansible --version               # expect: ansible [core 2.16.x], python 3.11+</div><p>Rules that prevent an entire class of failures:</p><ul><li>Keep the project <b>inside WSL home</b> (<code>~/ansible/</code>), not <code>/mnt/c/</code> — Windows mounts are slow and cannot hold Linux permissions (SSH keys at <code>0600</code> silently break there)</li><li>Use VS Code with the <b>WSL Remote</b> extension so editors save with LF endings; CRLF line endings corrupt YAML and vault files</li><li>Generate the SSH key <b>inside WSL</b> (<code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519</code>), copy the public half to servers with <code>ssh-copy-id</code></li><li>One <code>.venv</code> or <code>pipx</code> per toolset; never <code>sudo pip install</code> (breaks system Python)</li></ul><div class=\"code\">ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C \"ansible-control\"   # ~/.ssh/id_ed25519\nssh-copy-id ansible@10.0.1.11                # test login without password prompt\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m ping         # first proof from WSL</div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT CAN FAIL</div><ul><li><code>UNREACHABLE</code> → SSH key / WinRM creds / firewall</li><li><code>MODULE FAILURE</code> → Python missing on managed node</li><li><code>become</code> failure → sudoers / privilege escalation config</li></ul><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">9</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 02 · Architecture (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"agentless-control-to-managed-4\">AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">6 MIN</span></div></div><p>Each maps to troubleshooting in PDF3 Ch 12.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">REAL-WORLD CONNECTION</div><p>Fleet of 200 VMs: one control node (CI runner) holds inventory + Vault. No agent deployed to 200 hosts; rotation handled by SSH key management.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">Interview check</div><div class=\"m-exam\"><span class=\"tag\">Interview</span><b>Why is Ansible called agentless?</b><br />Managed nodes need no permanent Ansible daemon. Control node connects via SSH/WinRM per task and executes modules on demand.</div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Next</span>Architecture tells *how* Ansible reaches machines. Next: *which* machines — inventory and targeting.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">10</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 03 · Inventory</div><h1 id=\"inventory-which-machines\">INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">7 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"panel ar-panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">🇪🇬 الـ Inventory والاتصال: شرح عربي</div><div class=\"ar\">ملف <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Inventory</span> (مثل <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">hosts.ini</span>) يسرد عناوين <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">IPs</span> الـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Managed Nodes</span> مقسمة إلى <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Groups</span> مثل <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">[web]</span> و<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">[db]</span>، والمجموعة قد تكون أمًّا لمجموعات عبر <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">children</span> فيرث الداخل إعداداتها. جهّز الدخول بلا كلمة سر: ولّد <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Key Pair</span> بأمر <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ssh-keygen</span> (الخاص <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Private Key</span> يبقى سرًّا على Control Node)، انسخ العام بـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ssh-copy-id</span>، ثم جرّب <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ssh</span> مباشرة. متغيرات الوصول مثل <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible_host</span> و<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible_user</span> ومفتاح <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible_ssh_private_key_file</span> تشرح لـ«Ansible» كيف يصل، وخلف بوابة اقفز عبر <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ProxyJump</span>.</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Inventory</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Groups</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">hosts.ini</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">ssh-keygen</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">ssh-copy-id</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">ansible_host</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT — Inventory is target declaration</div><p>Inventory lists managed nodes and organizes them into <b>groups</b>. Playbooks select from this list; ad hoc commands address it directly.</p><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Inventory hierarchy</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">INVENTORY FILE</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">GROUPS<small>web · db · prod</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">HOSTS<small>web-01 · db-01</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">VARS<small>group_vars · host_vars</small></span></div></div><p>Static inventory example:</p><div class=\"code\">[web]\nweb-01 ansible_host=10.0.1.11\nweb-02 ansible_host=10.0.1.12\n\n[db]\ndb-01 ansible_host=10.0.1.21\n\n[prod:children]\nweb\ndb</div><p>Hierarchy:</p><div class=\"code\">INVENTORY\n  +-- groups (web, db, prod)\n  +-- hosts (web-01, web-02)\n  +-- group_vars / host_vars\n  +-- variables (ansible_user, ansible_become)</div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHY — Selection before declaration</div><p>Without inventory, automation has no audience. Inventory separates *which* from *what* — same playbook against <code>web</code> vs <code>prod</code> yields different blast radius.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">11</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 03 · Inventory (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"inventory-which-machines-2\">INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">7 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">HOW IT WORKS</div><ul><li><b>Static:</b> INI or YAML file, committed to repo, versioned.</li><li><b>Dynamic:</b> plugin queries cloud (AWS EC2, Azure) at runtime — generates inventory, not checked in.</li><li><b>Patterns:</b> <code>web</code>, <code>web[0]</code>, <code>web:db</code>, <code>prod:&amp;web</code>, <code>all:!db</code> — composed at CLI/play <code>hosts:</code>.</li><li><b>Variables:</b> inventory vars flow into variable precedence (taught fully in PDF2 Ch 06) via <code>group_vars/web.yml</code>, <code>host_vars/db-01.yml</code>.</li></ul><div class=\"taskhead\">PRACTICAL — Ad hoc proof (first execution)</div><p>Ad hoc = single task without playbook. Proves inventory + transport before YAML complexity.</p><div class=\"code\">COMMAND\n────────────────────────\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m ping</div><p>Meaning: run <code>ping</code> module against group <code>web</code>. Target: <code>web-01</code>, <code>web-02</code>. Internal: control node loads inventory, selects hosts, sends <code>ping.py</code> via SSH, returns JSON. Expected:</p><div class=\"code\">RESULT\n────────────────────────\nweb-01 | SUCCESS =&gt; { \"changed\": false, \"ping\": \"pong\" }\nweb-02 | SUCCESS =&gt; { \"changed\": false, \"ping\": \"pong\" }</div><p>Why it matters: proves selection, transport, Python on target in 5 seconds.</p><p>Common failure:</p><ul><li><code>UNREACHABLE</code> → check <code>ansible_user</code>, <code>ansible_host</code>, key, <code>ansible -m ping</code> with <code>-vvv</code></li><li><code>changed: false</code> surprise → <code>ping</code> is check-only; not a bug</li></ul><p>Second ad hoc:</p><div class=\"code\">$ ansible web -i inventory.ini -a \"uptime\"</div><p><code>-a</code> = <code>command</code> module args. Compare: <code>-m ping</code> vs <code>-a \"uptime\"</code> shows module vs raw command.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">12</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 03 · Inventory (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"inventory-which-machines-3\">INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">7 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">CONNECTING — SSH keys, users, IPs</div><p>Inventory names the targets; connection variables explain <b>how to reach them</b>. Four variables do 95% of the work:</p><div class=\"code\">[web]\nweb-01 ansible_host=10.0.1.11 ansible_user=ansible ansible_port=22\nweb-02 ansible_host=10.0.1.12 ansible_user=ansible\n\n[web:vars]\nansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/id_ed25519\nansible_become=true</div><ul><li><code>ansible_host</code> — real IP/hostname (the inventory name stays a stable alias even when IPs change)</li><li><code>ansible_user</code> — SSH login user; create a dedicated <code>ansible</code> user, never automate as <code>root</code> over SSH</li><li><code>ansible_port</code> — non-default SSH port where hardened</li><li><code>ansible_ssh_private_key_file</code> — which key; one key per environment (<code>prod_ed25519</code> vs <code>staging_ed25519</code>)</li></ul><p>Key bootstrap (once per server, from the control node):</p><div class=\"code\">ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/prod_ed25519   # private stays 0600, never leaves control node\nssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/prod_ed25519 ansible@10.0.1.11\nssh -i ~/.ssh/prod_ed25519 ansible@10.0.1.11   # must log in with no password prompt</div><p>Behind a bastion, hop transparently instead of copying keys around:</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">13</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 03 · Inventory (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"inventory-which-machines-4\">INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">7 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">CONNECTING — Bastions, first contact, trust</div><div class=\"code\">[web:vars]\nansible_ssh_common_args=-o ProxyJump=bastion.example.com</div><p>First-contact checklist (in order, stop at the first red):</p><div class=\"code\">ping -c2 10.0.1.11                             # IP reachable at all?\nssh ansible@10.0.1.11                          # key auth works by hand?\nansible web-01 -i inventory.ini -m ping        # Ansible transport works?\nansible web -i inventory.ini --list-hosts      # pattern selects who you think?</div><p>Host-key trust: first connection prompts <code>Are you sure you want to continue connecting?</code>. For known fleets, pre-seed <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code> with <code>ssh-keyscan</code>; setting <code>host_key_checking=false</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code> silences the prompt but also silences real machine-in-the-middle warnings — scope it to ephemeral lab inventories only.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT CAN FAIL</div><ul><li>Pattern selects zero hosts → <code>hosts: web</code> matches nothing if group misspelled → <code>--list-hosts</code> verify</li><li>Wrong vars file location → <code>group_vars/</code> must sit beside inventory file</li><li>Dynamic inventory creds → plugin fails silently if cloud creds missing</li></ul><div class=\"taskhead\">REAL-WORLD CONNECTION</div><p>Staging vs prod: same playbook, different inventories (<code>inventories/staging/</code>, <code>inventories/prod/</code>). Promotion = change <code>-i</code> path, not playbook.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">14</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 03 · Inventory (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"inventory-which-machines-5\">INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">7 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">LAB — Observe / Execute / Verify</div><p>Objective: prove inventory selection. Prereqs: inventory.ini above. Steps:</p><ol><li><code>ansible all -i inventory.ini --list-hosts</code> → Observe all targets</li><li><code>ansible web -i inventory.ini -m ping</code> → Execute + Watch CHANGED vs SUCCESS</li><li>Verify <code>pong</code>. Explain: <code>ping</code> validates transport, not config.</li></ol><p>Failure scenarios: wrong <code>ansible_host</code> → UNREACHABLE; fix → correct IP.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">Interview check</div><div class=\"m-exam\"><span class=\"tag\">Interview</span><b>Static vs dynamic inventory?</b><br />Static lists hosts in file; dynamic generates list at runtime from cloud API. Static for stable fleet, dynamic for elastic/autoscaling.</div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Next</span>We know *which* machines. Next: *what* state they should reach — playbooks.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">15</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 04 · Playbooks</div><h1 id=\"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml\">PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">8 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"panel ar-panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">🇪🇬 أول Playbook: شرح عربي</div><div class=\"ar\">الأمر السريع <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Ad-hoc Command</span> ينفّذ <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Task</span> واحدة بلا ملف (مثال: <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible all -i hosts.ini -m ping</span>)، وصيغته العامة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible group -i file -m module -a args</span>. أما <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Playbook</span> فملف <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">YAML</span> يحوي <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Plays</span>، وكل Play تستهدف مجموعة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">hosts</span> وتضم <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Tasks</span> يستدعي كلٌّ منها <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Module</span> واحدًا. لغة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">YAML</span> حساسة للمسافات: ابدأ بـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">---</span>، ممنوع <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Tab</span>، والتزم مسافتين، واستخدم أسماء كاملة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">FQCN</span> مثل <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ansible.builtin.package</span>. قبل التشغيل افحص بـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">--syntax-check</span> وعاين بـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">--check</span>.</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Ad-hoc Commands</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Playbook</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Tasks</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">YAML</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">FQCN</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">--check</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT — Playbook is desired-state document</div><p>A playbook maps <b>hosts</b> to <b>tasks</b> that invoke <b>modules</b> with <b>arguments</b>. It is YAML, declarative: describe end state, not step-by-step shell.</p><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Play nests tasks, tasks invoke modules</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk b-red\">PLAY<small>hosts · become</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">TASK<small>name</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk b-cyan\">MODULE<small>ansible.builtin.*</small></span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">ARGS<small>name · state</small></span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHY — From ad hoc to repeatable</div><p>Ad hoc proves one module once. Playbooks repeat, order, and audit multiple tasks. They are the unit committed to Git and triggered by CI.</p><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Ad hoc command</th><th>Playbook</th></tr><tr><td>Shape</td><td>One module call from the CLI</td><td>Plays and tasks in a YAML file</td></tr><tr><td>Repeat</td><td>Retype the command every time</td><td>Run the file again, identical result</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Shell history on one laptop</td><td>Git diff plus <code>--check --diff</code></td></tr><tr><td>Handoff</td><td>Tell someone the command</td><td>Commit <code>site.yml</code>; CI runs it</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">16</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 04 · Playbooks (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-2\">PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">8 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">YAML — Syntax before semantics</div><div class=\"code\">- name: Configure web server\n  hosts: web\n  become: true          # privilege escalation\n\n  tasks:\n    - name: Install nginx\n      ansible.builtin.package:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n\n    - name: Ensure nginx running\n      ansible.builtin.service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started\n        enabled: true</div><p>Hierarchy:</p><div class=\"code\">PLAY  (hosts: web, become: true)\n  +-- tasks\n       +-- TASK (name: Install nginx)\n            +-- MODULE (ansible.builtin.package, FQCN)\n            +-- ARGUMENTS (name, state)</div><p>Rule: FQCN always (<code>ansible.builtin.*</code>) — short names obsolete. YAML whitespace matters; 2-space indent.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">HOW IT WORKS — Execution</div><div class=\"code\">PLAY (web) → TASK 1 (package) → SSH → managed node → check nginx absent → install → CHANGED\n          → TASK 2 (service) → check stopped → start → CHANGED\n          → RESULT per host → NEXT PLAY</div><p>Play = which hosts; Task = what module with which args; Module = implementation that converges state.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">17</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 04 · Playbooks (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-3\">PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">8 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">PRACTICAL — Run it</div><div class=\"code\">COMMAND\n────────────────────────\nansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml</div><p>Meaning: execute playbook against inventory. Target: <code>web</code> group. Internal: load <code>site.yml</code>, select hosts, for each task send module, collect <code>changed/ok/failed</code>. Expected:</p><div class=\"code\">RESULT\n────────────────────────\nPLAY [Configure web server] ***\nTASK [Install nginx] *** changed: [web-01]\nTASK [Ensure nginx running] *** changed: [web-01]\nPLAY RECAP *** web-01 : ok=2 changed=2 failed=0</div><p>Why it matters: proves declarative convergence; re-run should yield <code>ok=2 changed=0</code>.</p><p>Common failure: YAML parse error → <code>ERROR! Syntax Error</code> at line; fix indent or missing <code>:</code>.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT CAN FAIL</div><ul><li><code>hosts: web</code> matches zero → <code>skipping: no hosts matched</code> → verify with <code>--list-hosts</code></li><li><code>become: true</code> without sudo → <code>Failed to escalate</code> → <code>ansible_become_user</code> / sudoers</li><li>Module args typo → <code>unsupported parameter</code> → check <code>ansible-doc ansible.builtin.package</code></li></ul><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">18</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 04 · Playbooks (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-4\">PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">8 MIN</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">REAL-WORLD CONNECTION</div><p>Team commits <code>site.yml</code> to <code>infra/</code> repo; reviewer sees <code>state: present</code> intent, not <code>apt-get install</code> imperative.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">LAB — Observe / Execute / Verify</div><p>Observe: <code>nginx</code> absent (<code>systemctl status nginx</code> fails). Execute: <code>ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check --diff</code> (dry run). Verify: <code>--diff</code> shows planned changes. Execute for real, verify <code>ok</code> with <code>changed=2</code>, re-run verify <code>changed=0</code>.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">Interview check</div><div class=\"m-exam\"><span class=\"tag\">Interview</span><b>Play vs task vs module?</b><br />Play selects hosts and holds tasks; task invokes one module with arguments; module does work on managed node and reports convergence.</div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Next</span>Playbooks declare state. Next: how modules enforce idempotency and report CHANGED vs OK.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">19</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 05 · Idempotency</div><h1 id=\"modules-and-idempotency\">MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">ADV</span></div></div><div class=\"panel ar-panel\"><div class=\"ptitle\">🇪🇬 الـ Modules و Idempotency: شرح عربي</div><div class=\"ar\">الـ<span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Module</span> وحدة عمل جاهزة (تثبيت حزمة، نسخ ملف، إدارة <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Service</span>)، بينما <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Plugin</span> يغيّر سلوك «Ansible» نفسه (اتصال، بحث، ترشيح). الخاصية الجوهرية <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Idempotency</span>: تكرار نفس <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Script</span> لا يغيّر شيئًا بلا حاجة — مثبت مسبقًا يعني <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">ok</span> (أخضر: <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Server</span> في Desired State) لا إعادة تثبيت، وتغيير فعلي يعني <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">changed</span> (أصفر)، وخطأ يعني <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">failed</span> (أحمر). فضّل <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">Modules</span> على <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">command/shell</span> لتحصل على Idempotency ودعم <span class=\"ten\" dir=\"ltr\">check mode</span> تلقائيًا.</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">Modules</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Plugins</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">Idempotency</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">ok</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">changed</span><span class=\"harrow\">·</span><span class=\"blk\">failed</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT — Module + ideal result</div><p>Module = unit of desired state. Takes args, checks current state, acts only if needed, returns JSON with <code>changed</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>msg</code>.</p><div class=\"grid2\"><div class=\"zone\"><div class=\"zlabel\">First run — CHANGED</div><div class=\"code mini\">Desired: nginx present ↓ Current: absent ↓ Install → CHANGED</div></div><div class=\"zone\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Second run — OK</div><div class=\"code mini\">Desired: nginx present ↓ Current: present ↓ No action → OK</div></div></div><p>Idempotency: same input applied twice → same end state, second run reports no change.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHY — Desired state ≠ re-run commands</div><p>Scripts re-run commands blindly. Modules compare desired vs current:</p><div class=\"code\">DESIRED STATE (state: present)\n       ↓\nCURRENT STATE (nginx absent? present?)\n       ↓\nCOMPARE → CHANGE OR NO-OP → RESULT (CHANGED / OK)</div><p>Without this, automation cannot be safely repeated for drift correction or retries.</p><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">20</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 05 · Idempotency (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"modules-and-idempotency-2\">MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">ADV</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">HOW IT WORKS — Visual</div><p>First run:</p><div class=\"code\">Desired: nginx present → Current: absent → Install → CHANGED</div><p>Second run:</p><div class=\"code\">Desired: nginx present → Current: present → No action → OK</div><p>Result states (always labeled with symbol+color, not color alone):</p><ul><li><code>OK</code> (green ●) — converged, no change</li><li><code>CHANGED</code> (blue ◐) — converged, did change</li><li><code>FAILED</code> (red ) — did not converge</li><li><code>SKIPPED</code> (gray ○) — conditional skipped</li><li><code>UNREACHABLE</code> (amber ) — transport failed</li></ul><div class=\"panel\"><div class=\"zlabel\">Desired vs current decides everything</div><div class=\"frow\"><span class=\"blk\">DESIRED</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk\">CURRENT</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk b-cyan\">COMPARE</span><span class=\"harrow\">→</span><span class=\"blk b-ok\">CHANGE or NO-OP</span></div></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">21</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 05 · Idempotency (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"modules-and-idempotency-3\">MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">ADV</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">PRACTICAL — Observe idempotency</div><div class=\"code\">- name: Idempotency demo\n  hosts: web\n  tasks:\n    - name: Create app directory\n      ansible.builtin.file:\n        path: /opt/app\n        state: directory\n        mode: '0755'</div><p>First run: <code>changed: [web-01]</code>. Second run: <code>ok: [web-01]</code>. Proof of desired-state model.</p><p>Check and diff modes:</p><div class=\"code\">$ ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini site.yml --check --diff</div><p><code>--check</code> dry-runs, <code>--diff</code> shows planned file/service changes. Use for approval gates.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">WHAT CAN FAIL — Common misreads</div><ul><li><code>OK</code> misread as \"nothing happened\" → actually \"already converged\" (GOTCHA callout below)</li><li>Module not idempotent by misuse → <code>command: apt-get install</code> always CHANGED vs <code>package:</code> converges → use <code>package</code>/<code>file</code>/<code>template</code> not <code>command</code>/<code>shell</code> where module exists</li><li><code>changed_when</code> abuse → masks real change signal</li></ul><div class=\"mark m-warn\"><span class=\"tag\">Gotcha</span>\"OK\" does not mean Ansible did nothing meaningful.\nIt means desired state already holds.\nAlways read result state pair: ok vs changed count.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">22</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Chapter 05 · Idempotency (cont.)</div><h1 id=\"modules-and-idempotency-4\">MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">ADV</span></div></div><div class=\"taskhead\">COMMAND + RESULT PATTERN</div><div class=\"code\">COMMAND\n────────────────────────\nansible web -i inventory.ini -m ansible.builtin.file -a \"path=/opt/app state=directory mode=0755\"\n\nRESULT\n────────────────────────\nweb-01 | CHANGED =&gt; { \"changed\": true, \"path\": \"/opt/app\", \"state\": \"directory\" }\n\nWHAT HAPPENED: file module ensured directory exists, created it.\nWHY IT MATTERS: proves CHANGED on creation, OK on repeat.\nCOMMON FAILURE: path parent missing → use state=directory (creates parents), mode typo → permission drift.</div><div class=\"taskhead\">COMPARISON — Module vs shell</div><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Module (<code>file</code>, <code>package</code>)</th><th><code>command</code>/<code>shell</code></th></tr><tr><td>Intent</td><td>desired state</td><td>imperative command</td></tr><tr><td>Idempotency</td><td>built-in compare</td><td>always CHANGED unless <code>creates</code>/<code>removes</code></td></tr><tr><td>Check mode</td><td>supported</td><td>skipped</td></tr><tr><td>Audit</td><td><code>diff</code> friendly</td><td>opaque</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"taskhead\">REAL-WORLD CONNECTION</div><p>Patch fleet: re-run same playbook weekly → CHANGED only where drift occurred. <code>changed</code> count becomes drift metric fed to observability.</p><div class=\"taskhead\">Interview check</div><div class=\"m-exam\"><span class=\"tag\">Interview</span><b>What makes a module idempotent?</b><br />It compares desired vs current state and acts only when different, reporting CHANGED when it acted and OK when already converged.</div><div class=\"mark m-key\"><span class=\"tag\">Next</span>Mechan mechanics mastered in PDF1. Next PDF: data — variables, facts, registers — that make modules vary per host.</div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">23</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet\"><div class=\"pghead\"><div class=\"hrow\"><div><div class=\"kick\">Appendix · Cheatsheet</div><h1 id=\"cheatsheet-fqcn-and-failures\">CHEATSHEET — FQCN AND FAILURES</h1></div><span class=\"hmeta\">FINALE</span></div></div><div class=\"grid2\"><div><h3 id=\"a-fqcn-use-always\"><span>A</span> FQCN — Use Always</h3><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Legacy</th><th>FQCN</th></tr><tr><td><code>yum</code>, <code>apt</code></td><td><code>ansible.builtin.package</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>template</code></td><td><code>ansible.builtin.template</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>service</code></td><td><code>ansible.builtin.service</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>file</code></td><td><code>ansible.builtin.file</code></td></tr></table></div></div><div><h3 id=\"b-failure-index\"><span>B</span> Failure Index</h3><div class=\"table-scroll\"><table><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Fix</th></tr><tr><td>UNREACHABLE</td><td>SSH key / ansible_host</td></tr><tr><td>Failed to escalate</td><td>become + sudoers</td></tr><tr><td>YAML error</td><td>ansible-lint</td></tr><tr><td>OK misread</td><td>already converged</td></tr></table></div></div></div><div class=\"frow\"><span>● OK — converged, no change</span><span>◐ CHANGED — converged, did change</span><span> FAILED — did not converge</span></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">24</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div><div class=\"sheet cover\"><div><div class=\"ckick\">End Foundations · Vol 1</div><div>VOL 2 — PLAYBOOK ENGINEERING</div><p>Variables, logic, templates and roles: one playbook behaving differently per host.</p></div><div class=\"folio\"><span>Foundations · Vol 1</span><span class=\"fnum\">25</span><span><span class=\"rok\">ok</span> <span class=\"rchg\">changed</span> failed=0</span><span><a href=\"#toc\">TOC</a></span></div></div>","toc":[{"id":"automationmanual","text":"AUTOMATIONMANUAL","level":1},{"id":"table-of-contents","text":"TABLE OF CONTENTS","level":1},{"id":"why-automation-beats-scripts","text":"WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS","level":1},{"id":"why-automation-beats-scripts-2","text":"WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS","level":1},{"id":"why-automation-beats-scripts-3","text":"WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS","level":1},{"id":"why-automation-beats-scripts-4","text":"WHY AUTOMATION BEATS SCRIPTS","level":1},{"id":"agentless-control-to-managed","text":"AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED","level":1},{"id":"agentless-control-to-managed-2","text":"AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED","level":1},{"id":"agentless-control-to-managed-3","text":"AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED","level":1},{"id":"agentless-control-to-managed-4","text":"AGENTLESS — CONTROL TO MANAGED","level":1},{"id":"inventory-which-machines","text":"INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES","level":1},{"id":"inventory-which-machines-2","text":"INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES","level":1},{"id":"inventory-which-machines-3","text":"INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES","level":1},{"id":"inventory-which-machines-4","text":"INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES","level":1},{"id":"inventory-which-machines-5","text":"INVENTORY — WHICH MACHINES","level":1},{"id":"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml","text":"PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML","level":1},{"id":"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-2","text":"PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML","level":1},{"id":"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-3","text":"PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML","level":1},{"id":"playbook-desired-state-in-yaml-4","text":"PLAYBOOK — DESIRED STATE IN YAML","level":1},{"id":"modules-and-idempotency","text":"MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY","level":1},{"id":"modules-and-idempotency-2","text":"MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY","level":1},{"id":"modules-and-idempotency-3","text":"MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY","level":1},{"id":"modules-and-idempotency-4","text":"MODULES AND IDEMPOTENCY","level":1},{"id":"cheatsheet-fqcn-and-failures","text":"CHEATSHEET — FQCN AND FAILURES","level":1},{"id":"a-fqcn-use-always","text":"A FQCN — Use Always","level":3},{"id":"b-failure-index","text":"B Failure Index","level":3}],"words":4185,"assets":[]};